<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friedrich Schiller</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1759">1759</a>–<a href="/years/1805">1805</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Friedrich von Schiller</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Friedrich Schiller was a German poet, dramatist, historian and philosopher. His first play, The Robbers (1781), was followed by Don Carlos, the Wallenstein trilogy, Mary Stuart and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-tell">William Tell</a>. He taught history at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> from 1789 and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> in 1799, where his friendship with Goethe defined Weimar Classicism. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ode-to-joy">Ode to Joy</a> was set by Beethoven in the Ninth Symphony.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrich-schiller.jpg" alt="Friedrich Schiller" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anton Graff · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anton_Graff_-_Friedrich_Schiller.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Johann Christoph Friedrich Schiller was born on 10 November 1759 in Marbach, in the Duchy of Württemberg, the only son of the military doctor Johann Kaspar Schiller and Elisabetha Dorothea Schiller. He grew up in a devoutly Lutheran family and spent much of his youth studying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. His parents wanted him to become a priest, and the village priest taught him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek. In 1773 he entered the Karlsschule Stuttgart, an elite military academy founded by Karl Eugen, Duke of Württemberg, and graduated as a doctor of medicine in 1780.</p><p>At the school he read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a> and Goethe and wrote The Robbers, a play about two aristocratic brothers, one of whom leads rebellious students into the Bohemian forest as Robin Hood-like bandits. Its critique of social corruption astounded its first audience and made him an overnight sensation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1780 Schiller became a regimental doctor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, a post he disliked. He left his regiment without permission to see the first performance of The Robbers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a>, was arrested, sentenced to 14 days of imprisonment and forbidden by the duke to publish further works. In 1782 he fled Stuttgart and went by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, Mannheim, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>. On 26 May 1789 he gave his inaugural lecture as professor of History and Philosophy at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a> and wrote historical works there. On 22 February 1790 he married Charlotte von Lengefeld.</p><p>From 1788 until his death he kept a productive but complicated friendship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe">Johann Wolfgang von Goethe</a>, who convinced him to return to playwriting. Together they founded the Weimar Theater and wrote the Xenien, satirical short poems aimed at their opponents. The Duke of Saxe-Weimar ennobled him in 1802.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Schiller's philosophical work was concerned above all with human freedom, a theme that runs through his histories and plays: the Wallenstein trilogy concerns the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, and Don Carlos the revolt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. His On the Aesthetic Education of Man, first published in 1794, was prompted by his disillusion with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and its descent into violence, and argues that a people's moral character can be raised by first touching their souls with beauty. He died of tuberculosis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> in 1805, aged 45. A skull long believed to be his was shown by DNA tests in 2008 not to be.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Friedrich Schiller” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the German Sturm und Drang, “storm and stress”, of the 1770s, young writers rebelled against rules in the name of passion and genius: Johann Wolfgang von Goethe’s The Sorrows of Young Werther of 1774 moved a generation of readers, and <b>Friedrich Schiller</b>’s The Robbers of 1781 shook audiences.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Friedrich Schiller</b> wrote &quot;An die Freude&quot; in the summer of 1785.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ode-to-joy">Ode to Joy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Duchess Anna Amalia and Charles Augustus the united duchy became the home of Goethe and <b>Schiller</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weimar">Duchy of Saxe-Weimar</a></li><li><blockquote>Duchess Anna Amalia, regent from 1758, invited Christoph Martin Wieland as tutor; her son Charles Augustus brought Goethe to Weimar in 1775, later followed by Herder and <b>Schiller</b>, while the University of Jena flourished.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach">Duchy of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Schiller</b> first met Goethe in Rudolstadt in 1788.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-schwarzburg-rudolstadt">Principality of Schwarzburg-Rudolstadt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Marbach am Neckar, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.94,9.26&amp;year=1788" aria-label="Marbach am Neckar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4764 455 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Friedrich Schiller"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5214 725h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5214 725h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.94,9.26&amp;year=1788">What was here in 1788? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Yes! there's a limit to the despot's power! When the oppressed looks round in vain for justice, When his sore burden may no more be borne, With fearless heart he makes appeal to Heaven, And thence brings down his everlasting rights…&#8221;</p><footer>Wilhelm Tell (1804), act 2, scene 2 &#183; tr. Theodore Martin, 1847 &#183; spoken by Stauffacher at the Rütli &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6788/pg6788-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But the strong man is the strongest when alone.&#8221;</p><footer>Wilhelm Tell (1804), act 1, scene 3 &#183; tr. Theodore Martin, 1847 &#183; spoken by Tell &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6788/pg6788-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…man only plays when in the full meaning of the word he is a man, and he is only completely a man when he plays.&#8221;</p><footer>Letters on the Aesthetic Education of Man (1795), letter 15 &#183; anonymous translation (Aesthetical Essays, Bohn edition, 19th century) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6798/pg6798-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;One pen-stroke now, One motion of your hand, can new create The earth! but grant us liberty of thought.&#8221;</p><footer>Don Carlos (1787), act 3 &#183; tr. Richard Dillon Boylan &#183; spoken by the Marquis of Posa to King Philip II &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6789/pg6789-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
